# snapd confined snaps with a seccomp allow-list. The default profile is a
# plain-text syscall list held in a Go raw string, and it mediated ioctl()
# with exactly one exception:
#
#     ioctl - !TIOCSTI
#
# meaning "allow ioctl() unless the request is TIOCSTI". TIOCSTI is the
# request that pushes a character back onto a terminal's input queue, so a
# snap that held CAP_SYS_ADMIN could type into another user's terminal. The
# console analogue TIOCLINUX (man ioctl_console) does the same for the
# virtual consoles, and the allow-rule admits it, so a malicious snap could
# inject input into the controlling terminal and have commands run outside
# the sandbox after the snap exits. The companion global blacklist
# (cmd/snap-seccomp-blacklist, C) had the same single-constant omission: it
# installs one seccomp_rule_add masked-equality rule for TIOCSTI and none for
# TIOCLINUX.
#
# The pool SHA d7b49dd6f59 is the last commit of fix PR canonical/snapd#12849
# ("many: add a bunch of TODO/FIXME for a followup :)"), which edits two
# comments and comments out one spread-test line -- no security logic. The
# advisory references only the PR, so the SHA is never verified against it.
# The metadata flags no commit. The fixing changes are on release/2.59,
# present on master: dddcfd6ac8daa84feb80eb6fd88f852ced70629c adds the
# explicit denies
#
#     ~ioctl - TIOCSTI
#     ~ioctl - TIOCLINUX
#
# to the template (the ~ prefix is the explicit-deny form e4681c57b
# introduced, because a second `ioctl - !CONST` allow-rule would let each
# rule admit the other's constant), and 52af545f3c0d8b086500ab86f1617039056
# adds the second seccomp_rule_add with datum_b = TIOCLINUX. Remediation
# verified present at both, and at master today. Recorded CWE-74 is the
# impact, not the mechanism: nothing is injected in this repository, the
# defect is a confinement filter that admits a request it should deny
# (CWE-284).
#
# ATTENTION, rechecked. Authorization-by-configuration family (1514, 1535,
# 1540, 1556, 1557, 1593) in its purest form: the control is a static policy
# blob and the remediation is a policy entry. There is no dataflow -- the
# attacker is a program outside this codebase and the sink is a filter
# compiled at runtime. Re-measured over three --cache off runs per side,
# stable and identical at both revisions: interfaces/seccomp 1 finding
# (VYQL-RES-001, backend_test.go:947), cmd/snap-seccomp-blacklist 2
# (VYQL-MEM-006 NullableDeref on the arch_set loop), cmd/snap-seccomp 0.
# The review-flag view adds three flags, all in backend_test.go
# (LockAcquire, LockRelease, ResourceRelease). The library profile, which
# adds six caller-controlled-input rows to that subtree, reports the same six
# at both revisions and all of them in backend.go/backend_test.go. `vyql
# match` on template.go reports no source, no sink and no control label --
# a real zero, not a vacuous one: the same command on backend_test.go in the
# same run lists FilePathAccess, UnsafeUpload and ResourceHandleSink labels.
#
# No vocabulary has landed for this class. code.NetworkFilteringDisabled is
# still the nearest CWE-284 filter-omission issue and still carries exactly
# three bindings -- python packet_filter_chain_default_deny, ruby m5a48bf47,
# go wireguardDeviceWithoutInputPacketFilter -- all network packet filters.
# Nothing shipped since the first pass adds a filter, policy, sandbox or
# syscall concept.
#
# The engine is not the blocker, and that is now measured rather than
# assumed. A throwaway presence binding on `node.context.literal contains
# "ioctl - !TIOCSTI"` emits code.NetworkFilteringDisabled at template.go:24,
# 616, 636 and 712 -- the four package-scope raw-string declarations in the
# file -- so a Go binding can read the policy blob's text. Two consequences:
# a future fix is a binding-plus-rule question with no engine work, and the
# Go literal token is not node-confined across package-scope declarations,
# so one policy line already labels four vars and any rule on it reports
# four rows.
#
# Both discriminating forms are measured and both are the fix's own shape:
#
#   - `... and not (node.context.literal contains "TIOCLINUX")` scores 4 at
#     dddcfd6ac^ and 0 at dddcfd6ac. The constant is the advisory's
#     inference (and flatpak's twin CVE-2023-28100): neither libseccomp's
#     documentation nor snapd's states that a terminal-mediation policy must
#     deny TIOCLINUX, so the binding fails the genericity test.
#   - `... and not (node.context.literal contains "~ioctl")` also scores 4
#     and 0, and names no constant -- but its meaning is the fixing
#     commit's reformatting, not the weakness. TIOCSTI was already denied at
#     the parent by the exception form; the fix's `~ioctl - TIOCSTI` line is
#     a redundant restatement added beside the real deny, so this form fires
#     on any template that uses only the exception spelling. That is the
#     fixing-commit-layout discriminator, and it would flag a correctly
#     enforcing profile written in the other form.
#
# Reachable set, re-enumerated by grep across the 284 sibling clones under
# /tmp/cve-work/rank*/: the `ioctl - !` allow-rule spelling occurs nowhere
# but snapd's template. TIOCSTI and TIOCLINUX elsewhere are syscall-table
# constant definitions (radare2 and rizin sflib headers, runc's vendored
# golang.org/x/sys zerrors) and seccomp_rule_add elsewhere is libseccomp's
# own wrapper or a comment beside it. A binding for this shape has a
# reachable set of one repository and no true positive to generalise to,
# which is the repro-shaped patch, so nothing is minted. No construct is
# mishandled and no ENGINE-GAPS.md entry is filed.
#
# Flip conditions: when a confinement-policy concept lands with a curated
# request set, the first test (the allow-rule with a single exception) and
# the third (the single masked-equality deny) must flip to expect, while the
# fixed forms and the minimal template that lists no ioctl rule at all stay
# reject.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap

test "snapd: default seccomp template allows ioctl with every request except TIOCSTI"
  lang go
  file template.go
  reject VYQL-CFG-015
  code
  ```
  package seccomp

  // defaultTemplate contains default seccomp template.
  // It can be overridden for testing using MockTemplate().
  var defaultTemplate = []byte(`
  # Description: Allows access to app-specific directories and basic runtime
  #
  # The default seccomp policy is default deny with a whitelist of allowed
  # syscalls. The default policy is intended to be safe for any application to
  # use and should be evaluated in conjunction with other security backends (eg
  # AppArmor). For example, a few particularly problematic syscalls that are left
  # out are: mount, ptrace, pivot_root, ...

  accept4
  access
  acct
  arch_prctl

  inotify_add_watch
  inotify_init
  inotify_init1
  inotify_rm_watch

  # ioctl() mediation currently primarily relies on Linux capabilities as well as
  # the initial syscall for the fd to pass to ioctl(). See 'man capabilities'
  # and 'man ioctl_list'. TIOCSTI requires CAP_SYS_ADMIN but allows for faking
  # input (man tty_ioctl), so we disallow it to prevent snaps plugging interfaces
  # with 'capability sys_admin' from interfering with other snaps or the
  # unconfined user's terminal.
  # TODO: this should be scaled back even more
  ioctl - !TIOCSTI

  io_cancel
  io_destroy
  io_getevents
  io_pgetevents
  io_pgetevents_time64
  io_setup
  io_submit
  ioprio_get

  kill
  link
  linkat

  lseek
  llseek
  _llseek
  `)

  // MockTemplate makes the seccomp code use a different template than the
  // default one for testing.
  func MockTemplate(tpl string) {
      defaultTemplate = []byte(tpl)
  }
  ```

test "snapd: template denies ioctl with TIOCSTI and TIOCLINUX explicitly"
  lang go
  file template.go
  reject VYQL-CFG-015
  code
  ```
  package seccomp

  // defaultTemplate contains default seccomp template.
  // It can be overridden for testing using MockTemplate().
  var defaultTemplate = []byte(`
  # Description: Allows access to app-specific directories and basic runtime
  #
  # The default seccomp policy is default deny with a whitelist of allowed
  # syscalls. The default policy is intended to be safe for any application to
  # use and should be evaluated in conjunction with other security backends (eg
  # AppArmor). For example, a few particularly problematic syscalls that are left
  # out are: mount, ptrace, pivot_root, ...

  accept4
  access
  acct
  arch_prctl

  inotify_add_watch
  inotify_init
  inotify_init1
  inotify_rm_watch

  # ioctl() mediation currently primarily relies on Linux capabilities as well as
  # the initial syscall for the fd to pass to ioctl(). See 'man capabilities'
  # and 'man ioctl_list'. TIOCSTI requires CAP_SYS_ADMIN but allows for faking
  # input (man tty_ioctl), so we disallow it to prevent snaps plugging interfaces
  # with 'capability sys_admin' from interfering with other snaps or the
  # unconfined user's terminal.
  # similarly, TIOCLINUX allows to fake input as well (man ioctl_console) so
  # disallow that too
  # TODO: this should be scaled back even more
  ~ioctl - TIOCSTI
  ~ioctl - TIOCLINUX
  # restrict argument otherwise will match all uses of ioctl() and allow the rules
  # that are disallowed above
  ioctl - !TIOCSTI

  io_cancel
  io_destroy
  io_getevents
  io_pgetevents
  io_pgetevents_time64
  io_setup
  io_submit
  ioprio_get

  kill
  link
  linkat

  lseek
  llseek
  _llseek
  `)

  // MockTemplate makes the seccomp code use a different template than the
  // default one for testing.
  func MockTemplate(tpl string) {
      defaultTemplate = []byte(tpl)
  }
  ```

test "snapd: global blacklist filter installs a single ioctl rule for TIOCSTI"
  lang c
  file snap-seccomp-blacklist.c
  reject VYQL-CFG-015
  code
  ```
  #include <errno.h>
  #include <inttypes.h>
  #include <seccomp.h>
  #include <sys/ioctl.h>

  static int populate_filter(scmp_filter_ctx ctx)
  {
      int sc_err;

      /* Resolve the name of "ioctl" on this architecture. */
      int sys_ioctl_nr;
      sys_ioctl_nr = seccomp_syscall_resolve_name("ioctl");
      if (sys_ioctl_nr == __NR_SCMP_ERROR) {
          showerr("cannot resolve ioctl system call number");
          return -ESRCH;
      }

      const struct scmp_arg_cmp no_tty_inject = {
          /* When faced with ioctl(fd, request); refuse to proceed when
           * request&0xffffffff == TIOCSTI. This specific way to encode the
           * filter has the following important properties:
           *
           * - it blocks ioctl(fd, TIOCSTI, ptr).
           * - it also blocks ioctl(fd, (1UL<<32) | TIOCSTI, ptr).
           * - it doesn't block ioctl(fd, (1UL<<32) | (request not equal to TIOCSTI), ptr); */
          .arg = 1,
          .op = SCMP_CMP_MASKED_EQ,
          .datum_a = 0xffffffffUL,
          .datum_b = TIOCSTI,
      };
      sc_err = seccomp_rule_add(ctx, SCMP_ACT_ERRNO(EPERM), sys_ioctl_nr, 1, no_tty_inject);

      if (sc_err < 0) {
          showerr("cannot add rule preventing the use high bits in ioctl");
          return sc_err;
      }
      return 0;
  }
  ```

test "snapd: blacklist filter adds a second ioctl rule for TIOCLINUX"
  lang c
  file snap-seccomp-blacklist.c
  reject VYQL-CFG-015
  code
  ```
  #include <errno.h>
  #include <inttypes.h>
  #include <seccomp.h>
  #include <sys/ioctl.h>

  static int populate_filter(scmp_filter_ctx ctx)
  {
      int sc_err;

      /* Resolve the name of "ioctl" on this architecture. */
      int sys_ioctl_nr;
      sys_ioctl_nr = seccomp_syscall_resolve_name("ioctl");
      if (sys_ioctl_nr == __NR_SCMP_ERROR) {
          showerr("cannot resolve ioctl system call number");
          return -ESRCH;
      }

      struct scmp_arg_cmp no_tty_inject = {
          /* When faced with ioctl(fd, request); refuse to proceed when
           * request&0xffffffff == TIOCSTI. This specific way to encode the
           * filter has the following important properties:
           *
           * - it blocks ioctl(fd, TIOCSTI, ptr).
           * - it also blocks ioctl(fd, (1UL<<32) | TIOCSTI, ptr).
           * - it doesn't block ioctl(fd, (1UL<<32) | (request not equal to TIOCSTI), ptr); */
          .arg = 1,
          .op = SCMP_CMP_MASKED_EQ,
          .datum_a = 0xffffffffUL,
          .datum_b = TIOCSTI,
      };
      sc_err = seccomp_rule_add(ctx, SCMP_ACT_ERRNO(EPERM), sys_ioctl_nr, 1, no_tty_inject);

      /* also block use of TIOCLINUX */
      no_tty_inject.datum_b = TIOCLINUX;
      sc_err = seccomp_rule_add(ctx, SCMP_ACT_ERRNO(EPERM), sys_ioctl_nr, 1, no_tty_inject);

      if (sc_err < 0) {
          showerr("cannot add rule preventing the use high bits in ioctl");
          return sc_err;
      }
      return 0;
  }
  ```

